%S A401497 1,3,7,13,21,31,43,57,73,91,111,133,157,183,211,239,269,301,335,371,
%T A401497 409,449,491,535,579,623,669,717,767,819,871,923,977,1033,1091,1149,
%U A401497 1207,1265,1325,1387,1451,1515,1579,1647,1719,1793,1869,1945,2023,2101,2181,2263
%N A401497 Place n points at the integer polar angles of the Archimedean spiral r = k, 0 <= k < n, then around the k-th point draw a circle whose radius is the distance from the k-th point to the (k+1)-th point: a(n) is the number of finite regions in the resulting planar graph.
%C A401497 For the first fifteen terms the number of regions follows the formula a(n) = A002061(n) = n^2 - n + 1. This is due to the n-th circle intersecting all previous circles and thus adding 2*(n-1) new regions. However when the 16th circle is added it does not intersect the 13th circle, see attached image. One can show the circumferences of these circles are apart by 0.027398181... . See the Formula and A401523. Beyond the 16th circle all subsequent additional circles do not intersect one or more previous circles, and from the 38th circle and beyond the n-th circle does not intersect the first circle at the origin with radius 1.
%H A401497 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401497/b401497.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..100</a>
